row('lem-surface-p-basis-subfield-separation','For k of characteristic p, A=k[[X_1,...,X_n]][Y_1,...,Y_m], K=Frac(A), there is a downward directed family K_J containing K^p with intersection K^p, with A finite over A_J=k_J[[X_1^p,...,X_n^p]][Y_1^p,...,Y_m^p]. For every finite L/K, intersection L^p K_J=L^p.',
'''Choose a p-basis b_i of k over k^p by Zorn. For finite J put k_J=k^p(b_i:i outside J). The p-monomials on J form a finite basis of k/k_J. Together with X- and Y-monomials of exponents <p they give an explicit finite basis of A over A_J. If f/g^p lies in every K_J, clearing its denominator in K_J gives b^p f in A_J. Every A_J-derivation therefore kills f. Partial derivatives in X,Y force their exponents to be divisible by p; coefficient derivations dual to each b_i force every coefficient into k^p. Thus f is a pth power, proving intersection K_J=K^p. Directedness uses union of finite J. For a downward directed family F_J with intersection F, a finite-dimensional vector subspace V of H^r meets every F_J^r nontrivially only if it meets F^r nontrivially: induct on r, choose an F_J so the subspace with first coordinate zero has zero intersection, normalize the remaining vector's first coordinate to 1, and use uniqueness and directedness to force its other coordinates into F. Applying this observation to relations among any finite p-independent list proves that this list stays p-independent over some K_J. For finite L/K, prove intersection L^p K_J=L^p by a finite tower: a separable simple step has a basis of pth powers, and intersection is coefficientwise; a degree-p inseparable step uses the finite-dimensional subspace observation on the spans of 1,t,...,t^(p-1). This avoids silently assuming these spans stay linearly independent over every K_J.''',labels=['lemma-p-basis','lemma-intersection-subfields-subspace','lemma-intersection-subfields','lemma-power-series-ring-subfields'],missing=['Map published p-basis/derivation definitions or supply their elementary definitions inside this lemma. AC is spent on the p-basis.'])
row('lem-surface-non-pth-power-detected-by-derivation','If B is a domain of characteristic p, finite type over a complete equicharacteristic Noetherian local ring, and f is not a pth power in Frac(B), some derivation D:B→B has D(f)≠0.',
'''Replace the base by its image, a complete local domain, and use its published finite power-series subring R. Generic Noether normalization gives R[Y]⊂B′⊂B with B′ finite over R[Y] and B′_g=B_g, g∈R nonzero: normalize over Frac(R), then replace each algebra generator x by g x to clear its monic equation. Replace f by g^(pN)f in B′. In L=Frac(B′), df is nonzero because the kernel of the universal absolute derivation is L^p (expand on a p-basis). The preceding separation lemma chooses J so df remains nonzero over K_J. A linear functional on the finite-dimensional differential space gives a K_J-derivation of L detecting f. Clear its values on finitely many A_J-module generators of B′ to obtain D′:B′→B′. Derivations extend to localization by D(a/s)=D(a)/s-aD(s)/s². If B′_g=B_g, multiply D′ by g^(N+1), where g^N times each B′-algebra generator of B lies in B′; the quotient formula proves that this derivation preserves B. Because the initial multiplier g^(pN) has derivative zero, the resulting derivation detects the original f.''',deps=['lem-surface-p-basis-subfield-separation','cor-complete-local-domain-finite-over-a-regular-power-series-ring'],labels=['lemma-find-D','lemma-derivation-extends'],missing=['The generic normalization reduction is proved here from published field Noether normalization; map its exact supplier ID.'])
row('lem-surface-derivations-and-regular-hypersurfaces','Derivations extend through localizations and Noetherian adic completions. If T is regular and D(f) is a unit, T[z]/(z^r-f) is regular.',
'''D(I^n)⊂I^(n-1) gives a compatible map on inverse limits, whose Leibniz identity follows modulo every I^n; the localization quotient formula gives the other extension. Extend D to T[z] with D(z)=0. At a local ring with maximal ideal n containing z^r-f, membership of z^r-f in n² would imply its derivative belongs to n, whereas that derivative is -D(f), a unit. Thus the equation is part of a regular system of parameters, and its quotient is regular. Polynomial regularity and localization regularity are needed here.''',deps=['thm-completion-preserves-regular-local-rings'],labels=['lemma-derivation-extends','lemma-quotient-regular','lemma-degree-p-extension-regular'],missing=['Map exact published regular-local parameter/regular-quotient and polynomial-regularity suppliers; completion regularity alone does not prove them.'])
row('lem-surface-finite-completion-factors','For a finite map R→S of Noetherian rings and p∈Spec(R), R_p^hat⊗_R S is the product of the maximal-adic completions S_q^hat for q over p.',
'''Finite-module completion is tensoring by R_p^hat. For each n, S_p/p^nS_p is Artinian and is the product of its localizations at the finitely many q over p; take inverse limits. In each factor the radical of pS_q is qS_q, so the two adic topologies are equivalent. Quotients commute with completion by exactness. In particular formal fibres for a finite ring extension are factors of residue-field base changes of the old formal fibres.''',deps=['thm-completion-of-a-noetherian-local-ring'],source='algebra.tex',labels=['lemma-completion-finite-extension'],missing=['Map finite-module completion tensor supplier and Artinian product supplier; proof is given, but these exact IDs were not located/read in this session.'])
row('lem-surface-generic-power-series-formal-fibres','For A=k[[X]][Y], every completed local generic fibre A_p^hat⊗_A Frac(A) is geometrically regular.',
'''It is regular before purely inseparable base change because A is regular and regularity survives completion and localization. In characteristic zero finite extensions are separable and preserve regularity. In characteristic p test finite purely inseparable extensions by degree-p towers M⊂M[z]/(z^p-f). Choose a finite A-subalgebra B⊂M with fraction field M and f∈B by clearing denominators with pth powers. B and B[z]/(z^p-f) have unique primes over p since their fraction-field extensions are purely inseparable. The finite-completion-factor lemma identifies A_p^hat⊗M with B_r^hat⊗_B M. Inductively it is regular. The detecting-derivation lemma gives D:B→B with D(f) nonzero; extend through localization, completion, and localization. Its value on f is a unit because it lies in M×. The hypersurface lemma proves regularity after the next degree-p step. This proves geometric regularity by the finite purely inseparable test.''',deps=['lem-surface-non-pth-power-detected-by-derivation','lem-surface-derivations-and-regular-hypersurfaces','lem-surface-finite-completion-factors','thm-completion-preserves-regular-local-rings'],labels=['lemma-helper-G-ring'],missing=['Finite purely inseparable testing and separable base-change preservation of regularity must be proved or mapped; they are not a consequence of the definition alone.'])
row('lem-surface-complete-equicharacteristic-formal-fibres','Every complete equicharacteristic Noetherian local ring has geometrically regular formal fibres at every localization.',
'''For q⊂p replace the ring by its complete local domain quotient by q. Its generic formal fibres suffice, since completion commutes with that quotient. Choose its published finite regular power-series subring. By the finite-completion-factor lemma each generic formal fibre is a factor of the corresponding geometric base change of a generic formal fibre of the power-series subring. The preceding lemma proves that fibre geometrically regular. Quotient, localization, and finite residue extensions preserve the indicated identifications.''',deps=['lem-surface-generic-power-series-formal-fibres','lem-surface-finite-completion-factors','cor-complete-local-domain-finite-over-a-regular-power-series-ring'],labels=['lemma-check-G-ring-easy','proposition-Noetherian-complete-G-ring'])
row('lem-surface-completed-polynomial-generic-fibre','For complete equicharacteristic local A, q maximal in A[t] over its closed point and r⊂q with r∩A=0 and r≠0, the fibre A[t]_q^hat⊗κ(r) is geometrically regular.',
'''The field κ(r) is finite over K=Frac(A). For every finite purely inseparable L/κ(r), choose finite A⊂A′⊂L with Frac(A′)=L. Since A′ is a complete local domain (finite algebra over complete local A, and a domain), finite completion factors reduce the problem to the case κ(r)=Frac(A). In that case rK[t]=(t-f), f∈K. Put T=A[t]_q^hat⊗_A K. Need T geometrically regular over K. Reduce first to a finite regular power-series subring A0 of A, and to factors of the finite base change of its completed polynomial local ring B0. B0 is regular, since A0[t]_q0 is regular. To prove B0⊗K0 geometrically regular in characteristic p, induct on finite purely inseparable extensions as in the generic-power-series lemma. A detecting derivation on a finite A0-subalgebra A1 extends to B0⊗A0 A1 because the completion of a polynomial localization is topologically formally smooth: apply its square-zero lifting property to the map a↦a+εD(a). The resulting derivation detects f, so each degree-p step is a regular hypersurface. In characteristic zero separable extensions suffice. Thus T is regular; ∂/∂t extends to T and sends t-f to 1, so the hypersurface lemma proves T/(t-f) regular. The argument also applies after the finite A′ reduction and hence gives geometric regularity.''',deps=['lem-surface-complete-equicharacteristic-formal-fibres','lem-surface-non-pth-power-detected-by-derivation','lem-surface-finite-completion-factors','lem-surface-derivations-and-regular-hypersurfaces'],labels=['lemma-another-helper-G-ring','lemma-lift-derivation-through-fs','proposition-fs-regular'],missing=['WRITE the continuous square-zero lifting proof for completed polynomial localizations and its finite base changes, including compatibility of inverse-limit lifts. The present argument isolates this exact unresolved obligation; do not cite general André or label this row closed.'])
row('lem-surface-finite-type-formal-fibres','Fields and complete equicharacteristic Noetherian local bases have geometrically regular formal fibres for every essentially finite-type algebra.',
'''A field is a complete local ring. A quotient has the property by completion/quotient compatibility. Reduce finite-type algebras to polynomial rings and induct on the number of variables. For the one-variable step, check local rings at maximal primes q. Put p=q∩R, localize R at p, and base change to its completion. The resulting completed polynomial local ring has the same completion as R[t]_q, as is seen modulo each power of q. Base change of R_p→R_p^hat is regular, and the preceding two fibre lemmas cover respectively the zero and nonzero generic primes after quotienting the complete base by their contraction. Thus the polynomial completion map is regular by composition and faithfully flat descent. To pass from maximal primes to all primes, choose P′ above P in R_m^hat, complete at P′, and use the complete-ring formal-fibre result and faithfully flat descent for R_P^hat→(R_m^hat)_(P′)^hat. Flatness here is the completion/base-change flatness; fibres descend because regularity descends along faithfully flat local maps. These are substantive permanence facts, not an inference from a graph.''',deps=['lem-surface-completed-polynomial-generic-fibre','lem-surface-complete-equicharacteristic-formal-fibres'],labels=['lemma-check-G-ring-maximal-ideals','proposition-finite-type-over-G-ring','lemma-regular-permanence','lemma-regular-composition'],missing=['Map/prove regularity faithful-flat descent and base-change/composition, and flatness of the map between the two completions in the maximal-prime reduction. Parent reports this route independently read; exact closure must remain explicit.'])
row('lem-surface-open-regular-locus','Every finite-type algebra over a field or a complete equicharacteristic Noetherian local ring has open regular locus.',
'''First every complete local domain has a nonempty regular open. It is finite over a regular power-series subring; induct on fraction-field degree. Separable steps become standard étale after choosing a separating primitive element and clearing its equation/discriminant denominators. Degree-p steps are B[z]/(z^p-b) on a nonempty open: a detecting derivation of B has nonzero D(b), and invert D(b) and the denominator identifying the actual finite algebra with this hypersurface. The hypersurface lemma gives regularity. For any finite purely inseparable extension of a residue fraction field of the complete base, choose a finite subalgebra in that field; it is a complete local domain and has a regular open. For a finitely generated field extension K/k, enlarge k and K by finite purely inseparable extensions until K/k is separably generated: choose a transcendence basis; adjoin pth roots of finitely many coefficients in the minimal polynomial of a degree-p inseparable generator, and pth roots of the basis variables; the inseparable degree strictly decreases, giving a finite induction. Therefore every finite-type domain over the complete base has a regular open: base change to this finite inseparable subalgebra, spread a separating transcendence basis plus primitive separable generator to a standard smooth open, and descend regularity by generic flatness (restrict the base also so the open map is faithfully flat). Finally for any prime P at which T_P is regular, choose generators f_i of P_P forming a regular sequence; their generation and regularity spread to an open. On V(P), a nonempty regular open of T/P lifts to a regular open of T by the regular-sequence quotient criterion. The regular locus is stable under generalization, and this condition on every irreducible closed subset implies openness by Noetherian induction.''',deps=['lem-surface-non-pth-power-detected-by-derivation','lem-surface-derivations-and-regular-hypersurfaces','thm-generic-flatness-morphisms'],labels=['lemma-complete-Noetherian-domain-J-0','lemma-J-2','lemma-J-0-goes-down','lemma-J-0-goes-up','lemma-intersection-regular-with-closed','proposition-ubiquity-J-2'],missing=['Map/prove standard étale spread for a finite separable fraction-field extension; separating-transcendence-basis spread; descent of regularity; and openness of regular-sequence condition. These are actual proof obligations rather than a claim that J2 is available by citation.'])
row('lem-surface-complete-equicharacteristic-finite-integral-closure','The integral closure of any complete equicharacteristic Noetherian local domain in a finite extension of its fraction field is finite.',
'''Use the finite regular power-series subring R. For a finite separable extension of Frac(R), choose an integral power basis and use the nondegenerate trace pairing: traces of products of integral elements are integral and belong to R because R is normal; invert one discriminant to place every integral element in one finite R-module. In characteristic p first pass to a finite normal envelope and its maximal purely inseparable subextension. Handle that subextension by degree-p induction. At a step, the preceding normalization B is finite over R, normal and Noetherian. For L=Frac(B)[z]/(z^p-b), b∈B not a pth power, choose D:B→B with D(b)≠0. If u=sum_(j≤i) a_j z^j is integral, differentiate u^p∈B. Its derivative is the pth power of sum_(j≥1) j D(b) a_j z^(j-1), up to the harmless factor D(b)^(p-1), so that sum is integral. Inducting on i gives D(b)^i a_j∈B; the constant coefficient follows by subtraction and normality. All integral elements lie in sum_(j<p) B D(b)^(-(p-1))z^j, hence form a finite B-module. Separable trace finiteness then applies to the normal envelope. Integral closure in the desired intermediate field is a submodule of its finite closure; transitivity of integrality identifies closure of the original domain with closure of R. No Tate or mixed characteristic input is needed.''',deps=['cor-complete-local-domain-finite-over-a-regular-power-series-ring','lem-surface-non-pth-power-detected-by-derivation'],source='algebra.tex',labels=['lemma-Noetherian-normal-domain-insep-extension','lemma-Noetherian-normal-domain-finite-separable-extension','lemma-domain-char-p-N-1-2'],missing=['Map exact normality of power-series ring and separable trace-pairing suppliers. Published polynomial normalization theorem proves the field case only, not this power-series case.'])
row('lem-surface-regular-fibres-preserve-normality','A flat map of Noetherian rings with regular fibres carries normality of the base to normality of the target. In particular a normal essentially finite-type local ring over the permitted bases has normal completion, which is a domain.',
'''At S_Q over R_P the published flat-local depth formula and the inequality dim(S_Q)≤dim(R_P)+dim(fibre) prove S2: the inequality follows by lifting a system of parameters from the base and fibre, which together generate an ideal primary for the maximal ideal. For height≤1 in S, going down bounds dim(R_P)≤1, and the fibre is a quotient of S_Q so also has dimension≤1; base and fibre are regular. Lift their regular systems of parameters. The published sequence-lifting lemma makes their concatenation regular; they generate the maximal ideal, so depth≤dimension≤embedding dimension forces regularity. Thus S is S2 and R1. For completeness, the domain-only published Serre criterion is not enough. An S2 ring is S1, hence has no embedded associated primes; with R0 it is reduced, by injecting into localizations at its minimal associated primes, all fields. Let t∈Q(S) be integral. It lies in every height≤1 localization, since they are regular local domains. Put I={a∈S:at∈S}. If proper, choose a prime minimal over I, of height≥2, and localize there. The localized I is primary for the maximal ideal; S2 supplies an S-regular sequence x,y in I by prime avoidance. From y(xt)=x(yt), regularity modulo x gives xt∈xS, and cancellation gives t∈S, contradiction. Hence S is integrally closed in Q(S). The idempotents of Q(S), one per minimal component, are integral and belong to S, so a local such S has only one component and is a domain. Apply this to the completion map using the preceding formal-fibre lemma and published faithful flatness.''',deps=['lem-surface-finite-type-formal-fibres','cor-flat-local-depth-additivity','lem-flat-local-depth-formula-regular-sequence-split','cor-serre-normality-criterion-two-directions','thm-completion-of-a-noetherian-local-ring'],source='algebra.tex',labels=['lemma-Sk-goes-up','lemma-Rk-goes-up','lemma-normal-goes-up-noetherian'],missing=['Map regular local CM and associated-prime/prime-avoidance suppliers. The proof explicitly includes the extension from domain Serre to general rings; no circular assumption that completion is already a domain.'])
row('lem-surface-finite-type-normalization-finite','Every integral finite-type algebra B over a complete equicharacteristic Noetherian local base has finite normalization; the same holds over a field and after localization.',
'''At every maximal ideal m of B, the preceding formal-fibre lemma implies that the completion of B_m is reduced: flatness embeds it into its generic fibre, which is geometrically regular and hence reduced. For a reduced complete equicharacteristic local ring T, its normalization in Q(T) is the product of the finite normalizations of its finitely many complete domain components, hence is finite by the complete-domain finiteness lemma. If C is the normalization of B_m, flatness embeds C⊗T into Q(T); it is integral, hence a T-submodule of that finite normalization and is finite. Faithful flatness descends its finitely many generators to C. Thus every B_m has finite normalization. J2 gives a nonzero f with B_f regular and normal. To globalize without silently assuming local finiteness is uniform: for every finite birational B⊂B′⊂Frac(B), B′_f is normal; its nonnormal locus is closed. Indeed S2 failures are detected by embedded associated primes of B′/fB′, and R1 failures by its finitely many height-one associated primes, so Serre gives a finite union of their closures. Its finite image Z_(B′) in Spec(B) is closed. For each m choose finitely many global integral elements generating the local normalization; the finite algebra they generate has m outside Z. Quasi-compactness gives finitely many such algebras; their composite has every localization normal, because at each point one selected algebra already equals the local closure. This composite is the normalization and is finite. Localization commutes with integral closure by clearing the denominators in a monic equation. The published finite-type-over-field theorem supplies the field case directly.''',deps=['lem-surface-finite-type-formal-fibres','lem-surface-complete-equicharacteristic-finite-integral-closure','lem-surface-open-regular-locus','thm-integral-closure-finite-finite-type-domain-over-field'],source='algebra.tex',labels=['lemma-analytically-unramified-easy','lemma-characterize-N-1','lemma-openness-normal-locus'],missing=['For nonnormal-locus proof map associated-prime finiteness and Serre criterion for arbitrary domain localizations. No claim of universal catenarity or mixed characteristic is made.'])
