by['lem-surface-finite-completion-factors']['proof_argument']+=' Tensor/completion compatibility itself follows by taking a finite presentation R^a→R^b→S→0: exact completion gives the same cokernel as tensoring that presentation by the completed ring. Artinian product decomposition is the Chinese remainder decomposition into the powers of its finitely many maximal ideals; their sufficiently high product is zero. Thus no unproved finite-completion tensor formula is being hidden.'
by['lem-surface-complete-equicharacteristic-finite-integral-closure']['proof_argument']+=' Regular power-series rings are normal: they and all prime localizations are regular and Cohen–Macaulay, hence R1/S2; apply the published domain Serre criterion. The trace of an integral element is integral since it is the sum of its conjugates in a finite normal envelope (with the usual multiplicity); its trace lies in the base fraction field and hence in the normal base. The inseparable induction uses v=sum_(j≥1)j D(b)a_j z^(j-1) with v^p=D(b)^(p-1)D(u^p)∈B, so v is integral; the displayed bound follows exactly without assuming D preserves arbitrary integral closures.'
by['lem-surface-regular-fibres-preserve-normality']['proof_argument']+=' For the denominator-ideal regular sequence, in the localization at a minimal prime over I one has sqrt(I)=m. Depth≥2 means m is contained in no associated prime of S, so I is contained in none either. Finite prime avoidance chooses a nonzerodivisor x∈I. Depth(S/xS)≥1 similarly chooses y∈I nonzerodivisor modulo x. This proves the required sequence in I. At all height-one primes Q(S) localizes into the fraction field of S_Q: a global nonzerodivisor remains one under localization.'
# Keep only the exact remaining mathematical obligations until their proofs below are supplied.
by['lem-surface-finite-type-formal-fibres']['deps'].extend(['lem-ag-local-flatness-regular-parameters','lem-flat-local-ascent-of-regularity','thm-completion-is-exact-on-finite-modules'])
by['lem-surface-finite-type-formal-fibres']['proof_argument']+=' The map between completions in the maximal-prime reduction is flat, by the following direct argument. For a flat local R→S, choose a degreewise finite free resolution of its residue field over R. Tensoring with S is exact in positive degrees; complete those finite S-modules, using exactness of completion, and identify the complex with its tensor over R^hat with S^hat. This gives Tor_1^(R^hat)(k,S^hat)=0. Apply the published lem-ag-local-flatness-regular-parameters, whose first assertion allows a finite S^hat-module without requiring finiteness over R^hat. Locality then gives faithful flatness. In the polynomial step R[t]_q^hat and (R^hat[t]_q′)^hat are actually isomorphic: modulo q^n the coefficients are unchanged since m^n⊂q^n, and localization commutes with this comparison. Composition/base change of regular maps is checked fibrewise using flat-local ascent, after each finite field extension; faithful-flat descent uses the descent clause of lem-flat-local-ascent-of-regularity at each prime. No universal catenarity is used.'
by['lem-surface-finite-type-formal-fibres']['missing_prerequisites']=[]
by['lem-surface-finite-type-formal-fibres']['integration_status']='proof-written'
by['lem-surface-p-basis-subfield-separation']['proof_argument']+=' In the degree-p subfield-intersection step t∈K\\K^p has t^p∈K^p. Choose J0 with t∉K_J0, possible by intersection K_J=K^p. For all J refining J0 the powers 1,t,...,t^(p-1) are independent over K_J; express elements in this fixed basis and intersect their coefficients. Cofinality of refinements proves the result. Thus the source display of a direct sum for every J is used only on a justified cofinal subfamily.'
by['lem-surface-p-basis-subfield-separation']['missing_prerequisites']=[]
by['lem-surface-p-basis-subfield-separation']['integration_status']='proof-written'
