row('lem-surface-geometric-regularity-field-test-and-generic-spread','For a Noetherian k-algebra T it suffices to test geometric regularity after finite purely inseparable field extensions. If K/k is finitely generated, finite purely inseparable enlargements K′/K and k′/k can make K′/k′ separably generated. A separably generated generic fraction-field extension of finite-type domains spreads to a standard smooth nonempty open.',
'''Choose a transcendence basis y for K/k and the maximal separable intermediate field M of K/k(y). If K/M has positive inseparable degree, choose β∈K\\M with α=β^p∈M. Write the separable minimal polynomial P of α over k(y). Adjoin to k pth roots of the finitely many coefficients of the numerator and denominator polynomials expressing coefficients of P, and replace each y by y^(1/p). All coefficients of P now have pth roots in k′(y^(1/p)); taking coefficient roots gives a separable polynomial Q whose pth power is P(T^p). Thus the unique pth root β of α lies in a separable extension of the new rational field: Q(β)=0 and Q′(β)≠0. In the compositum K′, the inseparable degree therefore decreases by at least p. Repeat finitely; degrees multiply and all extensions made are finite purely inseparable. In characteristic zero there is nothing to do. For separably generated K/k, choose its separating transcendence basis y and a primitive separable generator θ. Clearing finitely many denominators makes its monic minimal polynomial P have coefficients in R[y]_a for a domain base R with k=Frac(R). Invert P′(θ). The algebra (R[y]_a[θ])_(P′) is a standard smooth algebra over R, and lies inside K; its injection follows by monic polynomial division and minimality over k(y). Given any finite-type R-subalgebra S⊂K with fraction field K, choose a common open containing y,θ and their inverses: the elements y,θ are fractions of elements of S, so first localize S to contain them. Conversely each of its finitely many generators and inverted denominators is a fraction in the standard smooth algebra, so invert their finitely many denominators there. The resulting two localized algebras agree inside K and are a nonempty standard smooth open of S after possibly restricting R. Published standard-smooth flatness and geometric-fibre regularity, together with flat-local regular ascent, prove regularity over a regular base. For the field test, take arbitrary finitely generated E/k and finite inseparable E′/E, k′/k as above. By hypothesis T⊗k k′ is regular. The standard smooth fraction-field model of E′/k′ makes T⊗k E′ regular by smooth ascent and localization. The map T⊗k E→T⊗k E′ is faithfully flat; published flat-local regular descent makes T⊗k E regular. This proves the test and also stability under finitely generated residue-field extensions.''',deps=['lem-ag-standard-smooth-flatness','lem-ag-standard-smooth-regular-geometric-fibres','lem-flat-local-ascent-of-regularity','thm-localisation-and-polynomial-extension-of-regular-rings'],source='algebra.tex',labels=['lemma-make-separably-generated','lemma-geometrically-regular','lemma-geometrically-regular-descent'])
