by['lem-surface-open-regular-locus']['proof_argument']+=' Spreading a regular sequence is justified by its successive multiplication kernels: they are finite modules and vanish at the chosen prime, so one denominator kills each kernel; generation of P is spread by the same finite-module argument on P/(f_i). Regularity lifts from the quotient successively by the published regular-element lifting theorem. For generic-flat descent of a regular source open, apply generic freeness to that localized algebra over the domain base; it becomes a nonzero free module, hence faithfully flat (the algebra is nonzero and injection at the generic point guarantees at least one basis element). Published flat-local regular descent then applies. The topological criterion follows by Noetherian induction: on every irreducible closed set the regular locus is empty if its generic point is absent (generalization stability), and otherwise contains a nonempty relative open; induction on the remaining proper closed subset proves constructibility. A constructible generalization-stable subset of a Noetherian space is open, as its specialization-stable constructible complement is closed.'
